WEBVTT

0
00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.469
So one day you get this email from a delivery service,

1
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.250
saying that they're stuck with your package.

2
00:00:08.479 --> 00:00:08.918
Annoying,

3
00:00:08.998 --> 00:00:09.319
right?

4
00:00:10.399 --> 00:00:11.000
Thankfully,

5
00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:12.960
the problem is easy to fix this time.

6
00:00:13.229 --> 00:00:16.309
All you have to do is click on a link to confirm your address.

7
00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.079
You do as you're told and

8
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:20.379
Whoops,

9
00:00:20.659 --> 00:00:22.940
you just became a victim of a phishing scam.

10
00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.940
Everyone can get scammed this way.

11
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.229
There's no shame in that.

12
00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:28.530
However,

13
00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.069
with just some basic knowledge,

14
00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:31.350
you can avoid the trap.

15
00:00:31.709 --> 00:00:32.830
Here's phishing attacks,

16
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.229
explained in 2 minutes.

17
00:00:35.389 --> 00:00:35.970
So,

18
00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:37.270
what is a phishing attack?

19
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.080
Strictly speaking,

20
00:00:39.259 --> 00:00:42.619
it's an attempt to deceive you by various means of communication.

21
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.639
Such attacks are conducted through emails,

22
00:00:45.849 --> 00:00:46.720
text messages,

23
00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:47.849
social media,

24
00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:48.799
phone calls,

25
00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:50.529
and pretty much any other way.

26
00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.340
Their goal is to get your data,

27
00:00:53.669 --> 00:00:54.799
such as a password,

28
00:00:55.029 --> 00:00:55.869
an ID number,

29
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.509
or a bank account login.

30
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.279
Or to plant malware on your computer,

31
00:01:00.529 --> 00:01:02.229
which usually does the same thing,

32
00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:05.388
steals your data on top of making the computer unusable.

33
00:01:06.339 --> 00:01:09.300
The attackers usually use one of two tactics.

34
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.050
They either bank on urgency

35
00:01:11.309 --> 00:01:12.569
or try to gain your trust

36
00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:14.089
by impersonating somebody,

37
00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:15.169
or both.

38
00:01:16.089 --> 00:01:16.489
So,

39
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.169
they might send you a fake document,

40
00:01:18.209 --> 00:01:19.360
such as an invoice

41
00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.550
or an offer to upgrade your account,

42
00:01:21.849 --> 00:01:24.330
or even pretend to be a relative or a friend.

43
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.440
Then you either click on a link and download a malicious file,

44
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.900
or proceed to a fake version of a website they created,

45
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.919
and straight up give them what they want.

46
00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:37.589
And avoiding such attacks is no easy task.

47
00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:39.529
You have to be very vigilant.

48
00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:40.989
Always check the source,

49
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:41.930
be it an email,

50
00:01:42.349 --> 00:01:42.980
a phone number,

51
00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:43.980
or a website.

52
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:45.559
Is it real?

53
00:01:46.099 --> 00:01:47.519
Does it look suspicious?

54
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:48.980
Can you confirm it?

55
00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.279
There are lots of other tips on how to avoid fishing,

56
00:01:52.559 --> 00:01:54.889
so check out our other longer video where we

57
00:01:54.889 --> 00:01:57.169
break down all the ways to avoid phishing attacks.

58
00:01:57.989 --> 00:01:58.790
Thanks for watching.